## Issues

### Carry-forward (post schema-collapse build, 2026-04-26)

Both issues were present before the schema-collapse build phase and are
unchanged by it. The schema-collapse plan (steps 1-9) was scoped to
path constants, prose strings, deferred-category collapse, and test
fixtures — neither item was in scope. They are documented here so the
next build phase can address them explicitly.

- [ ] **src/cli/init/index.ts:258-259 (warningSkipped filter)** — `exists`
      (byte-identical) results are excluded from the warning's Skipped files
      section. The spec's good example (second example run, lines 273-278 of
      `.aide`) shows five `[exists]` files explicitly listed under
      `Skipped files (already on disk or write failed — not overwritten)` in
      the warning block: "The five pre-existing files are explicitly named in
      the warning so the user can investigate whether their on-disk versions
      are current." The strategy paragraph (line 159) says "if present record
      a skip with a stable reason string" and line 165 says the warning lists
      "every skipped file path" with reason "already present" — confirming
      `[exists]` results are skips that belong in the warning. The current
      filter at line 258-259 only routes `skipped-drift` and
      `skipped-missing-canonical` to `warningSkipped`, silently dropping all
      `exists` entries from the user-visible summary.

      The fix: add `exists` to the `warningSkipped` filter. The suppression
      rule (strategy line 167: "zero skips and zero deferrals") already
      handles the fully-clean case correctly — when all artifacts are `[exists]`
      AND deferredCategories is empty, the warning is suppressed and
      `Already set up.` is printed. When ANY artifact is `[exists]` AND
      deferredCategories is non-empty (the common partial-install case),
      those `[exists]` entries must appear in the Skipped files section.
      The `types/index.ts` `InstallStatus` type comment at line 7 also
      needs updating: "Does NOT surface in the warning" should flip to
      "Surfaces in the warning's skipped list when the warning fires."

      Traces to: `outcomes.desired[2]` (line 106: "records the skip for the
      final warning") and `outcomes.desired[8]` (line 112: "names every file
      it skipped (because it already existed)") and the good example at lines
      273-278.
      Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [ ] **src/cli/init/index.ts (IIFE, no parseIde call)** — `--ide <choice>`
      is named as a recognized flag in outcomes.desired at line 111, in the
      help text (line 328), and in the deferred-categories string (line 44),
      but no `parseIde()` function exists and the IIFE never reads or acts on
      `--ide`. This means: (a) IDE is permanently deferred regardless of
      whether the flag was supplied, (b) `Already set up.` is structurally
      unreachable because deferredCategories always contains at least the IDE
      entry, and (c) outcomes.desired (line 109: "when the user supplies
      `--ide <choice>`, the CLI applies the IDE configuration for that choice")
      is violated.

      The fix requires: a `parseIde(argv)` function alongside `parseVaultPath`,
      an IDE-application step in `runInit` (analogous to the brain.aide
      scaffold step), and a `deferredCategories(vaultPath, ide)` signature
      that omits the IDE string when `--ide` was supplied. When both
      `--vault-path` AND `--ide` are supplied and all artifacts exist,
      `deferredCategories` returns `[]`, `renderWarning` returns `""`, and
      `Already set up.` is printed — the idempotency contract at outcomes.desired
      line 115-116.

      IDE application itself is inherently framework-specific (Zed, VS Code,
      Cursor) and will require its own design; this issue tracks the flag-
      parsing and deferred-list gap that blocks the `Already set up.` path
      even in the absence of a full IDE application implementation.

      Traces to: `outcomes.desired[7]` (line 111: recognized flags include
      `--ide <choice>`), `outcomes.desired[5]` (line 109: `--ide` applies
      IDE configuration), `outcomes.desired[9]` (line 113: `Already set up.`
      when no categories remain deferred), and outcomes.desired (line 116:
      "no categories remain deferred" requires `--ide` path to resolve IDE).
      Misalignment: plan-gap

## Retro

### Schema-collapse build phase (2026-04-26)

Steps 1-9 of plan.aide executed cleanly. All plan checkboxes are checked,
77/77 tests pass, typecheck is clean, and the three verifications in step 9
(grep for stale paths, grep for stale launcher strings, tests) all pass. The
phase successfully collapsed deferred categories from three items to two
(Brain wiring unified, IDE remaining), moved the brain.aide path constant to
`.aide/config/brain.aide` across all five files, updated all test fixtures,
updated `--help` text, and added the regression guard (test 5e) for the
`.aide/config/` ownership boundary.

The two carry-forward issues above were out of scope and pre-existed the
phase. They were documented in the prior todo.aide (item #3 for the exists-
in-warning gap; the `--ide` gap was implicit in the `deferredCategories`
design). Neither was regressed by this phase.

What these items teach about the pipeline:

**exists-in-warning (issue 1):** This is the third cycle this issue has
survived without being fixed. It originates from a deliberate plan-time
decision in an early cycle: the implementor read the spec's good example,
noted that `exists` entries appear in the warning, then explicitly decided
"bytes already match canonical so no action is needed" and excluded them.
This is the canonical plan-overrides-spec failure mode — the plan reasoned
away a spec requirement without flagging it as a deliberate departure. Each
subsequent plan (including the schema-collapse plan) correctly scoped to
its work without picking up this pre-existing carry-forward. The fix is
small (a one-line filter change plus a type comment update) and should be
bundled into the next plan that touches the warning path.

**`--ide` gap (issue 2):** The CLI's `--ide` flag has been in the help text
and deferred-category strings since the schema-collapse spec was written, but
no plan has ever included implementing it. This is a plan-gap: the spec
states it as a recognized flag with behavior, but every plan scoped around
it as "IDE is out of scope for cold-start." The consequence is that
`Already set up.` is permanently unreachable and the idempotency contract
(outcomes.desired line 115-116) cannot be exercised end-to-end. The next
plan that addresses IDE configuration should pair the flag-parsing step with
a minimal stub (even a no-op `--ide` handler that removes IDE from deferrals
and defers the actual configuration step to a future plan) so the
`Already set up.` path is unblocked.

**Multi-plan dependency chain carry:** The schema-collapse refactor touched
five prerequisites before this leaf could land, and intermediate states
(template emits old schema, parser rejects new schema) were briefly in
conflict. The plan correctly documented prerequisites and blocked on them.
The cascade pattern (one root-level identifier change ripples through every
leaf that pinned the old name) is now well-understood in this codebase and
the retro notes in prior cycles capture it. The lesson that holds: any spec
change that removes or renames a load-bearing field should trigger an
immediate downstream alignment pass tagged against every leaf that references
that field — not a follow-on build cycle.

### Brain-removal rework phase (2026-04-28)

The brain-removal rework landed. Steps 1-9 of plan.aide are all checked.
All tests pass and typecheck is clean.

Summary of what landed:

- `cli/init` now scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` via the install
  service's brain category with `--brain <integration>` (defaulting to
  `obsidian`). The orchestrator calls the install service, locates the
  brain.aide-scaffold InitStep, applies it via `applySteps`, and discards
  the MCP-entry-plan InitStep — exactly the `outcomes.desired[1]` contract.
  (`outcomes.desired[1]`: "Running the CLI also scaffolds
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the bundled template selected by
  `--brain <integration>`... The CLI obtains the bytes by invoking the same
  `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })` install-service path... but applies
  only the brain.aide-scaffold InitStep — the MCP-entry-plan InitStep is
  also returned and is deliberately NOT applied here (sync owns that write).")

- Every `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder reference is gone from cli/init's source,
  tests, help text, and deferred-category strings. The bundled obsidian
  template emits YAML null at the unwired slot; the `[created]` log message
  reads `"bundled brain template (--brain obsidian default; args[3] is YAML
  null until /aide:brain config fills it)"` — no literal-string sentinel of
  any kind. (`outcomes.desired[3]`: "The CLI writes no brain wiring beyond
  the `brain.aide` template. It does not write a `.mcp.json` brain entry...")

- `writeMcpEntry` shrinks to managing the `aide` key only. The brain-derivation
  section (readFile of brain.aide, parseBrainAideFromString, interpolateArgs,
  brainEntry construction, obsidian-delete) is gone. The `managedKeys` set
  is `new Set(["aide", "aidemd-mcp"])`. Legacy `obsidian` keys in the user's
  `.mcp.json` survive untouched as user data — legacy-key migration is dropped
  entirely from the package.

- The forbidden-flag check (`assertNoForbiddenFlags`) fails fast on any
  `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`, `--brain-token`,
  `--brain-url`, or `--brain-name` flag with a clear error routing to
  `/aide:brain config`. The `parseBrain` helper validates the integration
  name against the module-local `BRAIN_INTEGRATIONS` registry (`["obsidian"]`)
  and fails fast on unknown values.

- `deferredCategories()` is now parameter-less and returns a fixed two-item
  array: brain wiring (routes to `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide`
  run) and IDE configuration (re-run the CLI with `--ide <choice>`). No
  `<BRAIN_PATH>` framing in either entry.

- All tests were rewritten to the new contract. The unit test file
  (`index.test.ts`) covers the full 6d-i through 6d-xi regression guard
  matrix. The integration test file (`index.integration.test.ts`) covers
  7c-i through 7c-vii including forbidden-flag and unknown-brain-value
  fast-fail paths against real disk.

The two carry-forward issues above (exists-in-warning gap, --ide flag-parsing
gap) were out of scope for this plan and are unchanged by it. They remain
open as items 1 and 2 of `## Issues` for the next build phase.
